WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  the  verbal abuse was the worst i've ever  heard  killed the police uh shame on you  and arrest your own and then to me  personally that i should be raped and  murdered  somebody said that to your face more  than one person said that to me yes  sergeant imogen hurst was right in the  thick of the crowd  at the sarah everard vigil she says  there were groups there  less concerned about peaceful observance  set instead  on confrontation it was just  very very violent there were a lot of  pushing and shoving i was personally  assaulted i was hit three times in the  chest  but a fortnight on from the events which  caused such widespread alarm  many who were there in the crowd  believed the police could have adopted a  more hands-off approach  they're not being held accountable for  what went down they're not being held  accountable for creating the situation  that they found themselves in  placing blame on us and others and the  women that  arrived on the night seems  seems quite evident to me to be evidence  of the systemic  misogyny within the met police her  majesty's inspector of constabulary  who looked in great detail at the way in  which the vigil was policed  said it had been extremely complex and  challenging  the police they conclude acted  appropriately and were not heavy-handed  for senior commanders here at scotland  yard this report  is an almost complete vindication of the  policing operation on the 13th of march  in fact some of the few criticisms in  the report  are mainly focused on the senior  politicians who were quick to condemn  the police tactics  people not just politicians but there  were others  need to be quite careful about jumping  to conclusions before they've got to the  bottom of facts  particularly of course because in doing  so there's always a danger that you can  undermine  public confidence in policing and just  make things more difficult in the future  the london mayor effectively the met  commissioner's boss  was one of the first to criticize the  policing of the vigil  at the time describing it as neither  appropriate  or proportionate however the home  secretary who described the scenes at  the time as upsetting  has now thrown her complete support  behind the police  quite frankly i was absolutely appalled  and sickened  to see the level of violence that was  being displayed to  police officers but whatever the  official report concludes  there's the public perception of  high-profile events like the clapham  vigil  and that can prove far more difficult to  change  mark white sky news

